            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    British Columbia (Attorney General) v. Council of Canadians with Disabilities, 2022 SCC 27, [2022] 1 S.C.R. 794; Wilkinson v. Downton, [1897] 2 Q.B. 57; <b>Bracklow v. Bracklow</b>, [1999] 1 S.C.R. 420; Droit de la famille — 251674, 2025 QCCS 4145; K.L.B. v. British Columbia, 2003 SCC 51, [2003] 2 S.C.R. 403; R. v. Kruk, 2024 SCC &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;McLachlin J. (as she then was) has similarly described marriage as creating “the interdependence of two co-equals”, giving rise to “expectations and obligations” between the spouses (<b>Bracklow v. Bracklow</b>, [1999] 1 S.C.R. 420, at para. 30). [105] Notwithstanding the union of interests in intimate partnerships and the 
                </p>
